      <section class="check-understanding">
        <exercise id="_8fd2bb2b-35a9-49b3-93e0-6b1010b68b27" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para/>
            <para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–3\).</para>
            <para>The correct answer is is \(–3\). The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–3\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_97021ac7-5208-432a-a6f5-432c6448485f" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para/>
            <para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), what is the \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(-5\).</para>
            <para>The correct answer is is \(n\). The \(y\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(-5\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_1d9cfcdf-595c-471d-9307-16dd20ce2f36" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para> For the equation \(y=\frac{1}{2}(x+3)^2−5\), does the graph open up or down?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient \(\frac{1}{2})\).</para>
            <para>Incorrect. The correct answer is Up.The graph opens upward. The squared term has a positive coefficient \(\frac{1}{2})\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="_16199d79-2505-408f-b4ff-e5b931fa83bb" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem>
            <para/>
            <para> For the equation \(y=-(x+100)^2+50\), what is the \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution>
            <para>Correct! The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–100\).</para>
            <para>The correct answer is is \(–100\). The \(x\)-coordinate of the vertex of this equation is \(–100\).</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
        <exercise id="a1771807-d7bb-465d-9019-eabb1fce4737" class="multiple-choice">
